GREP(1)

NAME
     grep - print lines that match a pattern

SYNOPSIS
     grep [-ivcnl] pattern [file ...]

DESCRIPTION
     Prints every line that contains the pattern, and nothing else. If
     no line matches, it prints nothing.

     -i     ignore the difference between capitals and small letters
     -v     print the lines that do NOT match
     -c     print only a count of matching lines
     -n     put the number of each line in front of it
     -l     print only the names of the files that have a match

     Put the pattern in quotes if it has spaces in it.

     Given more than one file, grep puts the file's name and a colon
     in front of each line it prints. A * in a file name stands for
     every name: /home/*/* is every file in every directory in /home.

EXAMPLES
     grep marge /usr/adm/usage.log
     grep -c ttyh0 /usr/adm/usage.log
     grep "Aug 25" /usr/adm/usage.log
     grep -i -l welcome /home/*/*

SEE ALSO
     cut(1), sort(1)
